details.raw_content:
\begin{abstract}
Accurate forecasting of river discharge is critical for water resources management and flood risk mitigation. This study develops a Random Forest regression model to predict daily discharge at USGS gauge 01014000 on the St. John River, Maine, based on lagged discharge measurements from an upstream gauge (01010000). We compiled daily streamflow records spanning January 1, 2015, to December 31, 2020 (n = 4,384) via the USGS Water Services API, then pivoted and cleaned the time series to produce continuous upstream and downstream flow records. Feature engineering generated three lagged upstream flow predictors (t–1, t–2, t–3 days), and missing values were linearly interpolated. The dataset was split chronologically (80 % training, 20 % testing), and a RandomForestRegressor (100 trees, random_state=42) was trained to map upstream features to downstream discharge. Model performance on the held‐out test set achieved a Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency of 0.9004 and a low root‐mean‐square error, indicating excellent agreement between observed and predicted flows. Visual evaluation included scatter plots and hydrograph overlays for the test period. These results demonstrate the potential of lagged‐flow Random Forest models for operational streamflow forecasting in regulated and unregulated river systems.
\end{abstract}

\section{Conclusion}

In this study, we developed and evaluated a Random Forest regression model to forecast daily discharge at USGS gauge 01014000 on the St. John River using lagged flow observations from the upstream gauge 01010000 over the period 2015–2020. Data were acquired via the USGS Water Services API, cleaned and pivoted to create continuous upstream and downstream time series, and enhanced with three lagged upstream features to account for travel‐time dynamics. A chronologically split dataset (80 % training, 20 % testing) was used to train a 100‐tree Random Forest (random_state = 42), yielding a Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency of 0.9004 and a low RMSE on the independent test set. These metrics indicate that the model explains over 90 % of the variance in observed downstream discharge and exhibits minimal bias across a range of flow conditions.

The successful application of simple lagged‐flow predictors and an ensemble learning algorithm highlights the effectiveness of data‐driven approaches for river discharge forecasting without requiring extensive physical parameterization. By automating data retrieval and feature generation, the workflow is readily adaptable for near‐real‐time operational forecasting in both regulated and unregulated river systems. Future work should explore the integration of meteorological covariates, cross‐basin transferability assessments, and comparisons with alternative machine learning architectures to further enhance forecast skill and robustness.

