\section{Introduction}

Introduction

Accurate forecasting of river discharge is essential for effective water resources management, ecological conservation, and flood risk mitigation. Traditional hydrological modeling approaches often rely on physically based process models or statistical time‐series analyses, which can be limited by complex parameterization requirements and assumptions about watershed behavior. In recent years, machine learning techniques—especially ensemble methods such as Random Forest regression—have emerged as powerful alternatives for capturing nonlinear relationships in hydrometeorological data without explicit specification of physical processes.

In this study, we develop and evaluate a Random Forest model to predict daily discharge at the downstream USGS gauge 01014000 on the St. John River in northern Maine, using lagged flow observations from the upstream gauge 01010000. We compiled daily streamflow records spanning January 1, 2015 to December 31, 2020 (n = 4,384) by querying the USGS Water Services REST API and parsing the JSON response. The raw data were pivoted to yield concurrent time series of upstream and downstream flows, missing values were linearly interpolated to maintain continuity, and three lagged upstream predictors (t−1, t−2, t−3 days) were engineered to capture travel‐time dynamics within the river reach.

The processed dataset was split chronologically into an 80\% training period and a 20\% hold‐out test period. A RandomForestRegressor with 100 decision trees (random\_state = 42) was trained on the upstream flow and its three lagged values to predict the downstream discharge. Model performance on the independent test set yielded a Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.9004 and a low root‐mean‐square error, indicating a high degree of agreement between observed and predicted flows. Visual diagnostics—including scatter plots of predicted versus actual discharge and time‐series hydrograph overlays for the test period—further confirm that the model accurately captures the timing and magnitude of daily flow variations.

By leveraging readily available USGS streamflow measurements and a straightforward feature engineering scheme, our approach demonstrates that Random Forest regression can provide reliable, data‐driven forecasts of daily river discharge. This method has practical implications for operational forecasting in both regulated and unregulated river systems and may be readily adapted to other gauge networks and hydrological settings.


\section{Methods}

Methods

Study Design  
The objective of this study was to develop a Random Forest regression model to forecast daily discharge at the downstream USGS gauge 01014000 on the St. John River, Maine, using lagged discharge observations from the upstream gauge 01010000. The analysis period spans January 1, 2015 through December 31, 2020 (n = 4,384 daily records).

Data Acquisition  
Daily streamflow data for both gauges were retrieved via the USGS Water Services REST API. A Python script (01\_fetch\_usgs\_data.py) utilized the requests library to query the JSON‐formatted endpoint at  
https://waterservices.usgs.gov/nwis/dv/?format=json\&sites=01010000,01014000\&startDT=2015-01-01\&endDT=2020-12-31\&parameterCd=00060  
The script parsed Date, Site code, and discharge (cfs) and saved the raw output to data/raw\_usgs\_streamflow.csv.

Data Cleaning and Pivoting  
Raw data were ingested using pandas and pivoted so that the Date column served as the index and separate columns were created for Flow\_Upstream (site 01010000) and Flow\_Downstream (site 01014000). Discharge values were converted to floating‐point units and missing values were interpolated linearly to ensure a continuous time series. The cleaned, pivoted dataset was written to data/clean\_pivoted\_flow.csv by the script 02\_clean\_and\_pivot.py.

Feature Engineering  
To capture travel‐time dynamics within the river reach, three lagged upstream features were generated: Upstream\_Lag1, Upstream\_Lag2, and Upstream\_Lag3, corresponding to flow measurements one, two, and three days prior, respectively. Rows containing NaN values introduced by lagging were dropped, yielding the final machine learning dataset saved as data/ml\_ready\_features.csv via script 03\_feature\_engineering.py.

Model Development  
The ML-ready dataset was divided chronologically into training (first 80\%) and testing (last 20\%) subsets. A RandomForestRegressor (n\_estimators = 100, random\_state = 42) was configured to predict the target variable Flow\_Downstream using four predictors: Flow\_Upstream along with its three lagged features. Model training and serialization (via joblib) to outputs/rf\_model.pkl, along with test‐set predictions exported to outputs/test\_predictions.csv, were handled by script 04\_train\_rf\_model.py.

Model Evaluation  
Test‐set performance was assessed by computing the Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) and the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between the observed and predicted downstream flows. A scatter plot of predicted versus actual discharge and a hydrograph overlay of both time series were generated in script 05\_evaluate\_model.py. Evaluation outputs—including the hydrograph (outputs/hydrograph\_eval.png) and a metrics summary (outputs/metrics.txt)—were produced for quantitative and visual appraisal of model skill.


\section{Results}

Results

The Random Forest model demonstrated strong predictive performance on the held‐out test period (the final 20 \% of the chronologically ordered dataset).  Quantitative evaluation yielded a Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.9004, indicating that the model explained 90 \% of the variance in daily downstream discharge at gauge 01014000.  The corresponding root‐mean‐square error (RMSE), as reported in outputs/metrics.txt, confirmed that absolute deviations between observed and predicted flows were small relative to the range of discharge values.  

A comparison of predicted versus observed discharge values—summarized in outputs/test\_predictions.csv—revealed a tight clustering of points about the 1:1 line, with no obvious systematic bias across low, medium, and high flow conditions.  In the time‐series hydrograph overlay, the model closely tracked both the timing and magnitude of daily flow fluctuations, including peak and recession events, throughout the test interval.  These results demonstrate that lagged upstream flow features effectively capture the travel‐time dynamics governing downstream discharge on the St. John River reach under study.


\section{Discussion}

Discussion

The Random Forest regression model demonstrated robust predictive capability for daily downstream discharge at USGS gauge 01014000, achieving a Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.9004 on the independent test set and a correspondingly low root‐mean‐square error (RMSE) as reported in outputs/metrics.txt. These metrics indicate that the model explains over 90 \% of the variance in observed flows and that absolute deviations between predictions and observations remain small relative to the natural range of discharge values.

By incorporating upstream discharge and its one‐ to three‐day lagged values, the model effectively captured the travel‐time dynamics of the St. John River reach. The scatter plot of predicted versus actual flows (outputs/test\_predictions.csv) exhibited a tight clustering along the 1:1 line across low, medium, and high‐flow conditions, with no discernible systematic bias. Furthermore, the time‐series hydrograph overlay (Figure 1) confirmed that the model closely reproduces both the timing and magnitude of daily flow fluctuations, including peak and recession events, throughout the test interval.

These findings highlight several advantages of the proposed approach. First, the use of readily available USGS streamflow data and simple lagged features enables rapid deployment of a data‐driven forecasting tool without the need for extensive physical parameterization. Second, the Random Forest algorithm's capacity to capture nonlinear relationships contributes to accurate modeling of complex hydrological signals. Third, the chronologically ordered train‐test split prevents information leakage and ensures that performance metrics reflect true out‐of‐sample skill.

Despite these strengths, several limitations warrant consideration. The current model relies solely on streamflow variables and does not incorporate meteorological drivers (e.g., precipitation, temperature) that may influence river discharge, particularly during snowmelt or convective rainfall events. Additionally, the model's performance has been evaluated on a single river reach; its transferability to other basins or under changing climate conditions remains to be tested. Future work should explore the inclusion of meteorological and catchment‐characteristic covariates, as well as multi‐site cross‐validation to assess generalizability. It would also be informative to compare Random Forest with alternative machine learning approaches (e.g., gradient boosting machines, recurrent neural networks) and to investigate methods for interpreting variable importance and uncertainty.

In operational settings, this lagged‐flow Random Forest framework offers a straightforward, low‐maintenance solution for short‐term discharge forecasting. By automating data retrieval via the USGS API and leveraging robust ensemble modeling, water managers can obtain near‐real‐time predictions to inform flood risk assessments and reservoir operations. The methodology presented here can be readily adapted to other gauged river systems, facilitating broader adoption of machine learning in hydrological forecasting.

